2. the important parameters of print()

the print() has several useful parameters. the most important are sep and end.

2.1 the "Sep" parameter 

sep means separator. It controls what appears between multiple values.

Code:

print("Python","Java","C++",sep="&")

Output:

Python&Java&C++

another example (CODE) :

print(10,20,30,sep=" | ")

output:

10 | 20 | 30

Very important --> Sep is a single space.

2.2 the "end" parameter

Very Very important ---> by default, print() moves to a new line after displaying its output.

Code:

print("Hello")
print("world")

Output:

Hello
world

Important --> print() moves to new line after displaying the output.

We can change this behaviour with "end" parameter.

Code:

print("Hello", end="")
print("world")

Output:

HelloWorld

Code:

print("Hello", end=" ")
print("World")

Output:

Hello World

VERY IMPORTANT --> notice that gap between quotation marks in end.

Code:

print("Loading",end="...")
print("done")

Output:

Loading...done

Remember: 

sep: controls the space or separator between values.
end: controls what appears after the printed output.

2.3 VERY IMPORTANT --> Combining sep and end 

Code:

print("A","B","C",sep=":",end="!")
print("Python")

Output:

A:B:C!Python

Remember --> Notice the gap between quotation marks.

This is useful when formatting output exactly as required.

3. input in python: input()

The input() functions take information from the user through the keyboard.

Code:

name=input("Enter your name:")
print("Your name is", name)

output:

Enter your name: naman
Your name is Naman

Let's understand each line:

1. input("Enter your nam: ") displays a prompt and waits for you to type something.
2. when you press Enter, python returns the entered text.
3. name=stores that text in the variable name.
4. print() dispalys the stored value.

3.1 Taking input without a message.

you can also write:

Code:

name=input()
print(name)

Output:

we enter-> Naman
Naman

The program waits for input even though no prompt is diplayed.

3.2 VERY important --> input() returns a string 

Code:

age=input("Enter your age: ")
print(type(age))

Even if you type a number, for example, say 22, it returns as following.

<class 'str'>

Why ? Because input() returns a string, even if the user enters the number.

VERY IMPORTANT Code:

a=input("Enter the first number:")
b=input("Enter the second number:")
print(a+b)

Output:

Even if you enter 10 20 ...it result as following

1020

Python joins the strings instead of adding the numbers.
